it's always nice to get packages in the  mail and this one is from book  depository  so i've got a pretty good idea of what  this is this is something that i've been  looking forward to having my own copy of  for quite some time  let's check whether i'm on track or  mistaken or not and then i'll tell you  why this book is so particularly  important if i've actually predicted the  right book  so  let's see what we have  yeah there we go it is margaret  stoicism  and emotion very nice cover there isn't  it so this is a copy of a text that i've  had a copy out from the library for  quite some time  and then it got recalled i need it for  my  own research i actually worked through  this entire text with a  client and developed writing prompts and  all sorts of other cool stuff  so let me tell you those stories in just  a moment first i'm going to tell you why  this is such an important work for me so  this is like the definitive work on the  topic of stoicism  and the emotions it's not the only work  out there there are plenty of academic  articles  and plenty of books that discuss the  emotions in a stoic perspective but  this is really like the gold standard  right  and it's uh you know it's it's  margaret graver so a great scholar  on stoicism and hellenistic philosophy  in general a translator i got to meet  her briefly at stoicon  back in toronto in 2017  great conversations with her i've  interacted with her a few other times  and this is the sort of book that  when you read it as a scholar you say  man i wish i could have written this  book because it is so good so it's got  these uh that's right nine chapters to  it  and it's really well  organized it's not just very competent  and well researched and well thought out  it brings up some  really interesting questions topics  sometimes even conundrums right so it  begins with a science of the mind the  pathetic syllogism  vigor and responsibility feelings  without a sense these are all chapter  titles right brutishness and insanity  traits of character the development of  characters city of friends and lovers  and then there's the final chapter the  tears of alcibiades if you know who  alcibiades was he was sort of like the  bad boy of ancient athens  not a person who had a lot of control  over his emotions and then there's this  really interesting  appendix the status of confidence tauros  in greek in stoic classifications that  is is like  extra material that didn't quite make it  into the chapters but really needed to  be discussed  extensively footnoted i mean this  these are the notes  and then we have a bibliography so you  can tell how much of the book is  actually like deep deep end notes  great bibliography here it's well  indexed and so again the definitive work  on stoicism and emotions now why did i  need to have a copy of this book besides  the fact that it's cool to have you know  stuff on hellenistic philosophy and  stoics so i use this pretty extensively  in my own work  on stoicism and not just the emotions  but a number of other topics  i had a copy  from the marquette library that i've had  out for about three years and i just  kept renewing it because i had  what's called a courtesy appointment  think of it sort of like you're a  professor in town and they want to  recognize you and make it possible for  you to use the research libraries  holdings and nobody else was requesting  this book  but there was kind of a snafu and things  got mixed up in terms of my courtesy  appointment and all of the books that i  had out all 16 of them most of which  were on stoic philosophy  got recalled and so i got this email  from a librarian saying you got to bring  all these books in buddy and so i did  and we  we went through them and i was like i  need these for my research  um i don't know if this is going to get  fixed anytime soon we really pro we  probably need to buy our own copies and  so i got some alexander vaphredesius  and some simplicius and  a book by cicero the on the moral ends  that i also had out and then this is one  of the ones this is the last one to  arrive  from that batch and now that i've got it  i don't have to take it out from anybody  else i've got my own  copy if i ever run into margaret graver  again in the flesh i'll ask her to sign  it for me  i will mention that i have a student a  tutorial student that several years ago  i worked through  the entire book line by line with  because he really wanted to understand  it and i said this is a really great  great text  and so i highly recommend it to you it  is written in an academic fashion but  graver  writes in a way that's pretty accessible  for non-academics  and she provides good examples which is  very important  and  you know i think if you're interested in  stoicism or you're interested in the  emotions even if you're not interested  in stoicism in particular  this would be a book that you would want  to check out because there's so many  insights that she is bringing to the  fore  that are coming from stoic philosophy  and she's doing an excellent job in  connecting the dots so to speak among  the the stoic tech she also is drawing  heavily  on texts by people who are not stoics  but are responding to stoics and through  whom we we learn quite a bit about the  stoics like cicero who's pretty  receptive to them or galen who tends to  think that chrysippus and the other  stoics got some things fundamentally  wrong and there's others along the way  plutarch as well  so all told i am pretty happy with with  getting this copy i think you can tell  by my enthusiasm in here this is a cool  thing to have  my own copy of i've only seen it in  hardback before i kind of like having it  in soft cover it's got that nice  new  soft cover feel to it and a beautiful  cover design as well so i'll stop  talking here  if you're into these kind of books you  might want to get your hands on it if  not well at least you heard a good story  about me unboxing a book i was looking  forward to  you
 

